Review design dependence and the scope of finite evidence (@inquiry-design-review: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Distinguish-error-correction-uncertainty-and-variation.lingua#r_0045C26C08EFF750F1BF509B4E") { r_0B79BA988B7379FFA3C6499DD5
Reconstruct the complete investigation from a blank page. Start with the question and target, distinguish physical quantity from indication, identify observation and allocation units, and state the difference between randomly selecting units and randomly assigning conditions. Explain what nuisance factors the design addresses and why repeated readouts do not create additional independent assignments.

**Demonstrate finite reasoning:** build an outcome set and justified weights; define events, complements, intersections and conditional probabilities; derive the balanced and paired allocation counts; and show a dependent example whose marginals are identical. Define a quantity on outcomes, expectation, variance and their units. Prove linearity of expectation without assuming independence and identify the extra cross term in the variance of a sum. Contrast these probability models with the hard bounds used in the engineering block.

**Defend the comparison:** define the six-unit average effect, explain the unobserved potential response, and derive unbiasedness using the assignment indicators. Recover both design variances and explain the particular pairing's advantage. Show why an unbiased estimate can be far from its target in one realization. Explain what changes when assignment is deliberate, units interfere, observations are missing, or protocol conditions vary with time. Do not claim that enumeration establishes generalization to a wider population.

**Criticize inference:** state the sharp null, actual assignment mechanism, statistic and tail rule. Explain why holding outcomes fixed is justified under that null and why a zero-average-effect null is different. Reproduce 2/20 and prove the finite threshold bound with ties. Explain why this two-sided design cannot reject at 0.05, why non-rejection is not proof of no effect, and why a probability statement cannot replace the effect in units or the practical question.

**Independent work:** design a different small comparison with a benign measurable outcome. Give a predeclared allocation scheme, analysis and exclusion policy, then analyze a clearly labeled synthetic table before proposing an actual experiment. Have another reader reproduce the arithmetic and identify a rival explanation. Teach the conditioning or unit-of-assignment distinction using a new example, elicit a prediction and revise the explanation when it fails. Preserve provenance, code, source editions and unresolved limitations using [Make an argument understandable challengeable and preservable](Make-an-argument-understandable-challengeable-and-preservable.lingua#r_094622380644F206A98B67A516) and [Specify which result another investigator can reconstruct](Specify-which-result-another-investigator-can-reconstruct.lingua#r_0B84B9BB7199B19E8B2B61EF3F).

The common inquiry core is still incomplete. Continuous probability, estimation and interval interpretation, richer experimental and observational designs, uncertainty propagation, model criticism, causal assumptions, scholarly synthesis, learning/teaching and durable knowledge stewardship remain. This block provides finite foundations rather than a compressed substitute for those subjects. All six areas now have second foundation blocks; compare their actual preparation before the next common pass. Begin the next mathematics block with change and accumulation, then bring the other areas forward at comparable depth before specialized leaves.

**Return:** [Scientific inquiry and practice](../Inquiry.lingua#r_75MNKZQKNB0XBG021SWWW2HASB).

**Continue in the third common pass:** [Distinguish error correction uncertainty and variation](Distinguish-error-correction-uncertainty-and-variation.lingua#r_0045C26C08EFF750F1BF509B4E). Comparable mathematics, physics, chemistry, computing and engineering blocks now supply preparation; the earlier paired-measurement and allocation studies remain unchanged.

